{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_vrtx_01YKnJTZTKKAJjRmemoazhAW","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Q12 is done. The **Next** section now has only the validate step remaining. That's the next plan item — not this run's task (one task per run). \n\nHere's what was written for **Q12: Quantization — tradeoffs between size, speed, accuracy?**\n\n**Key content:**\n- **Format comparison table**: BF16 (baseline) → FP8 (H100, <1% loss) → INT8 (1–2% loss, throughput) → INT4/AWQ (0.5 bytes/param, 2–5% general, 10–20% math/code on small models)\n- **GPTQ vs AWQ**: GPTQ uses Hessian-based layer-by-layer rounding; AWQ is activation-aware — protects the ~1% of weights responsible for outlier activations, generally beating GPTQ at 4-bit\n- **Concrete example**: Llama 3 70B goes from 140GB (BF16, doesn't fit 2×A100) → 35GB (AWQ W4A16), with 1.6× decode throughput gain because decode is memory-bandwidth-bound\n- **Task sensitivity**: RAG generation at T=0 is forgiving at 4-bit; math/code (GSM8K, HumanEval) can drop 5–15 points on models <13B\n- **Key pitfalls**: Conflating QLoRA (NF4 during training) with GPTQ/AWQ (PTQ for inference); assuming quantization helps prefill as much as decode; calibration dataset mismatch"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":373,"cache_read_input_tokens":65537,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":373,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"05d52b7b-d946-4d18-9463-b60bea0474b0","uuid":"e2d6e9ae-8286-49fd-9534-eb1aee90598b"}
